course: dictionary
lesson: 4187
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «Gondolas» is one of these words. Read the words around it first, because they show you which meaning fits.
explain: «Venice in Italy is built on more than a hundred small islands in a lagoon, so it has canals instead of many of its streets, and people travel by boat, including the long black gondolas.» The clues are canals, streets and travel by boat. Here a gondola is a boat.
explain: In the other sentence, «gondolas packed side by side» on the Grand Canal fits boats tied up on the water. Ask: which meaning fits every clue in the sentence? If your meaning makes the whole sentence sensible, you have found the right one.
fact: A gondola is a long, narrow, flat-bottomed boat, rowed by one person standing at the back with one oar.
fact: Gondolas are a famous sight on the canals of Venice in Italy, a city built on water.
fact: A gondola can also be a cable car cabin that carries skiers up a mountain.
fact: A gondola can also be a basket hanging under a hot-air balloon, or a free-standing set of shelves in a supermarket.
fact: When a word has more than one meaning, the context shows you which meaning the writer chose.
exercise: «Venice has canals instead of many of its streets, and people travel by boat, including the long black gondolas.» What is a gondola in this sentence? | *A long, narrow boat rowed with one oar | A set of shelves in a supermarket | A basket under a balloon | A cabin hanging from a cable up a mountain
exercise: «Skiers rode up the mountain in a gondola.» What is a gondola in this sentence? | A set of supermarket shelves | A basket under a balloon | *A cabin hanging from a cable | A long boat on a canal
exercise: Which clue in «people travel by boat, including the long black gondolas» tells you a gondola is a boat? | *travel by boat | Venice | islands | lagoon
test: «The shop had a gondola of tins near the door.» Which meaning fits? | A long boat on a canal | A cabin on a mountain | *A free-standing set of shelves | A basket under a balloon
test: «Rowing the gondola along the canal, the man stood at the back with one oar.» Which meaning fits? | *A long, narrow boat rowed with one oar | A basket under a balloon | A set of shelves in a shop | A cabin hanging from a cable
exam: Why does context matter when a word has more than one meaning? | It means the word has no meaning at all | *It shows which meaning fits the words around it | Only a dictionary can help | It makes the word longer
